From 65137942adfbf8e2292a889d596eedab0458a8f9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ash Pera <ashley.pera@noaa.gov>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 21:39:37 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 07/13] Scalar transport and physics: remove roundoff negatives
 without adding mass

Two clips remove negative scalars, and each adds mass:
- max(0, .) in the copy back of the monotonic transport;
- where (scalars_2 < 0) scalars_2 = 0 in atm_srk3 before the physics.
With the two previous commits the transport is positive definite (the
monotonic one, and the positive-definite option), so what reaches the
clips is roundoff. On the supercell the clips added about 1e-20 kg/m2 per
10 minutes at dt = 8 s. With both limiters off (config_monotonic =
config_positive_definite = false, an explicitly unbounded scheme), the
second clip added 1.40 kg/m2 in 2 h.

Fix: a column-local guard, atm_nonneg_column, replaces both clips:
- In owned cells, the negative values are set to zero and the same mass is
  taken from the column's positive values in proportion to their mass
  (NCEP Office Note 506, eqs. 10-11).
- The plain clip is kept where borrowing is wrong or undefined. In those
  cases the result is bit for bit the old clip's (max(0, .), or the where
  clip):
  - a column without positive mass to borrow from (its added mass is
    returned, for metering);
  - a column with a NaN or Inf value, or with a weight that is not
    positive and finite. NaN and Inf propagate exactly as before, and no
    non-finite value enters any sum;
  - halo cells, where rho_zz_int is not computed;
  - TKE, which is not a conserved quantity. The other transported scalars
    (moisture species, number concentrations, aerosol numbers) are all
    conserved mixing ratios.
- A column without negative values gets the old clip, bit for bit. There
  is no reduction across cells or tasks.
- On OpenACC builds, rho_zz is updated on the host before the physics-side
  guard, which reads it.
- Unlike the earlier design, the monotonic copy back again guarantees a
  non-negative result in every column. The physics-side clip exists only
  in builds with DO_PHYSICS.

Unit tests (the red team's harness, adapted to this interface, with the
code extracted verbatim):
- Random columns are conserved to 1.8e-15 and leave no negatives.
- Columns without positive mass, NaN, Inf, -0.0, zero or overflowing
  weights and denormals all give the old clip's result. None of them
  returns a non-finite added mass.
- 400,000 halo/TKE columns with NaN, Inf and -0.0 match the old clips bit
  for bit.
- 400,000 columns without negatives are bit for bit unchanged.
- A decomposition emulation (1, 12, 16 tasks; strips and blocks) gives
  bitwise-identical results. The earlier domain fallback differed by 1 ulp
  in 7-8 of 200 trials.

This guard is not bit-for-bit neutral. In owned columns, roundoff negatives
(about 1e-20 kg/m2 per 10 minutes at dt = 8 s) are now borrowed instead of
zeroed. In the earlier runs of the same guard, Kessler differed by 1e-11
relative at 2 h, and Thompson's differences grew like those of a known
roundoff twin. Commits 1 and 2 alone change nothing where they do not
trigger.

Expected effect with both limiters off: what is left is the mass the plain
clip adds in columns without positive mass of a species. The earlier
supercell runs metered 0.33-0.36 kg/m2 by 2 h (0.25 of the precipitation),
down from 1.40.
Staged, not yet run: the supercell matrix (dt = 12 s and 8 s monotonic,
positive definite, both limiters off, Kessler), the red team's
decomposition (1, 12, 16 tasks), restart and dt = 20 s runs, and this
guard's meter.

AI-assisted: written with Claude Code (Anthropic); reviewed by the author.
